  "summary": "Haplotype-resolved assemblies of wild and related plant germplasm are now paired with graph and association analyses to resolve trait-linked variation.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is haplotype-aware genome representation applied to wild and related plant germplasm. The Carya glabra preprint reports a first haplotype-resolved, chromosome-scale assembly of an autotetraploid, with organelle genomes and annotation. The Aegilops caudata study pairs genome assembly and optical mapping with k-mer GWAS across 95 accessions, identifying stem rust resistance gene SrAect1. The Vitis work assembles four chromosome-scale diploid genomes and applies sequence graph analysis to map PdR1 haplotype diversity and candidate defense genes against Pierce's disease. What is new is the direct linkage of haplotype-aware representation to trait variation, through graph-based analysis in Vitis and assembly-plus-k-mer association in Aegilops. The Carya summary reports assembly and annotation only. The items do not share one organism or trait, so the shared element is methodological.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-12-29"
}
